# Chapter 9: subgradients via mollifiers

**Theorem 9.67** for `f : E → ℝ` strictly continuous at every point, `E` a finite-dimensional
real inner product space with an additive Haar measure `μ` (Lebesgue measure on `ℝⁿ` in the
book), and a bounded mollifier sequence `ψ` as in 7.19 (`IsBoundedMollifierSequence`), with
averages `f^ν = mollifiedAverage μ (ψ ν) f`, `f^ν(x) = ∫ ψ^ν(z) f(x - z) dz`:

* every `f^ν` is differentiable with `∇f^ν(x) = ∫ ψ^ν(z) ∇f(x - z) dz`, formula 9(38)
  (`hasGradientAt_mollifiedAverage`; only boundedness and measurability of the kernel are
  used, `∇f` existing almost everywhere by Rademacher's theorem 9.60);
* for continuous kernels `f^ν` is `C¹` (`contDiff_mollifiedAverage`), and `f^ν → f` uniformly
  on compact sets (7.19(a));
* the set `G(x̄)` of cluster values of `∇f^ν(x^ν)` along subsequences with `x^ν → x̄`
  (`mollifierGradientLimits`, see `mem_mollifierGradientLimits_iff`) satisfies
  `∂f(x̄) ⊂ G(x̄)` and `con ∂f(x̄) = con G(x̄)`, hence `∂f(x̄) = G(x̄)` at points of
  subdifferential regularity (`mollifier_subgradients`).

The inclusion `∂f(x̄) ⊂ G(x̄)` follows the book: a proximal subgradient `v̄` makes
`f - ⟪v̄, · - x̄⟫ + ρ|· - x̄|²` grow quadratically away from `x̄`; minimizers of its averages
over a small closed ball converge to `x̄`, where the gradient of the averaged quadratic tends to
`v̄`; 8.47(a) and closedness of the outer limit `G` then give all limiting subgradients.
For `con G(x̄) ⊂ con ∂f(x̄)` the averaged difference quotients are bounded by the Clarke
derivative `d̂f(x̄)`, so `G(x̄)` lies in `∂̄f(x̄) = con ∂f(x̄)`; this makes the book's second
(mean-value) half of 9(40) unnecessary once `∂f(x̄) ⊂ G(x̄)` is known.  `G(x̄)` is nonempty and
compact (`mollifierGradientLimits_nonempty_compact`).

The last assertion of 9.67, `v ∈ ∂̄f(x̄)` if and only if `∇f^ν(x̄) → v` for the averages of some
bounded mollifier sequence, is `mem_clarkeSubdifferential_iff_exists_mollifier`; the sequence
can be chosen continuous (`exists_continuous_mollifier_tendsto_gradient`).  As in the book, a
point of `G(x̄)` generated by `∇f^ν(x^ν)` is generated at `x̄` itself by the translated kernels
`ψ^ν(· + x^ν - x̄)`, and mixtures of kernels give convex combinations of gradients
(`exists_mixed_tendsto_gradient`); continuous kernels exist (`standardMollifier`, normalized
smooth bumps).
-/

